														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-342144">
									

<p>I am going to be a bit harsh here because I think, given this is your second try, you deserve to know: you REALLY need to work on using a clinical tone.</p>
<p>Some hints on maintaining a clinical tone.</p>
<p>1. Do not draw conclusions: present the evidence instead. For instance, you don't need to comment that a guy whose birth and death dates are 300 years apart is weird: people should get that just by reading. You don't need to speculate that the item is gloating, let the reader draw their own conclusion.</p>
<p>2. Do not use run-on sentences: each sentence should present one, or at most two, facts, FULL STOP. Then start a new sentence. If you have a list of similar things to talk about, use bullet points.</p>
<p>3. Do not go into details on side situations such as other SCPs involved: refer to the other page, or just say Data Expunged.</p>
<p>4. Each paragraph should be based on a single thesis.</p>
<p>5. Check your damn spelling and capitalization.</p>
<p>6. Finally, one issue I've found with all your SCPs to date: STICK TO ONE IDEA. Don't toss everything in there just because it's scary. SCP 173 is so effective because it's simple; a statue that kills you if no one looks at it. Try to be like that.</p>
<p>This article is seriously under-par. I am downvoting it until it becomes better.</p>

