<p>i was actually going to try to refute all of clef's points and offer a opposite opinion (and then leave the site, don't worry, I'm not one of those guys who will hold completely differing ideas against the consensus and then remain, stubbornly refusing to change, continuing to post articles that annoy everyone, and being a general pain in the side. i have more style then that) but then i saw how excellent the rewrite was, and that gave me hope. Or maybe i just have more patience then i thought. (no need to huff, "why does he need patience, he's the one trying us" i don't mean i need patience for the people here, but more for the frustration caused by the disparity between my expectations of everyone loving my articles, and what actually happed (getting severely down rated.. ouch!) A lesser men would of become ill tempered by now (or maybe a lesser men would of said a lesser man would of become ill tempered by now, and then act self righteous that he was actually taking everything in stride :)</p>
<p>Ok, anyways, one thing that really stuck with me were the bullets. I don't think theres anything wrong with my sentence structure besides being longer( and can someone tell me if there is? from what I've seen from the comments, a lot of people say they like the idea in a strange way "from what i could gather" "from what i saw". Is that the same as saying "from what i understood"? is my writing complete gibberish? i can handle my formatting not being appropriate for this site, but it would really disturb me if my writing is universally bad) but see now that the bullets are a much better way to do things.</p>
<p>I was debating leaving out "the team ventures to guess that this is it's way of gloating" but i decided that i really wanted to point people in that direction of thought. Was it so wrong to keep that in their. Can't something be clinical (lacking emotion) while still drawing hypothesis? i mean their scientists, right. thats what scientists do. (this is one of the points i was going to say to clef, although in a more likely ruder and huffier manner (at least thats how it sounded in my head when planning it out) (i'm sorry clef, your doing a great job, and I'm really thankful for your help, but when people stumble across their article with -3, strange thoughts kick around in their heads while the blood drains from their face)</p>
<p>well, i go nothing else to say right now. I'm going to attempt to become better (don't worry, after reading this comment and slap your head "he hasn't learned anything, look at it it's all one giant run on sentence" i always write like this when informal, and don't even try to make sense of it, it's all stream of conscious) and go with the flow. i hope this all works out, because i got some really sweat ideas coming up.</p>
<p>P.S</p>
<p>Calothisosi of Britannica is a made out figure, a sort of fake Celtic hero type. It stands to reason that we wouldn't know all the immortals who existed so while reading the article we could hear of one that we're never heard of before, so i just made him up. also i couldn't think of a "real" immortal who was invulnerable. there's a small list of real life immortals on wikipedia "list of immortals" is anyones interested.</p>
<p>Whats are you talking about when you mention Jesus' two graves? is it one of your ideas? because in mine, jesus christ was only implied to be in the trophy crypt (which i think is a great ending line. I'm glad you didn't take it out. really creepy. sums up the hopelessness. this thing kills even the divine, or maybe even reached into heaven.)</p>
<p>Kill the easter bunny instead. santa's actually pretty cool, the easter bunny on the other hand, is the dumbest idea in the world. maybe we could have it show up in the graveyard and replace it with a giant chicken, which makes marginally more sense.</p>

